WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.560
If cancer is genetic, why is it usually not inherited?

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.920
Watch one pale tissue cell as a clay-colored DNA change appears.

3
00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:13.180
That cell divides, and the change follows its daughters.

4
00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:15.880
Nearby cells lack it.

5
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:22.000
This is a somatic change, acquired during life, not a change carried in every cell.

6
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:29.010
The change matters only if it changes the lineage's future.

7
00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:32.790
A driver lets cells divide more or die less.

8
00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:37.410
Its branch widens while other branches continue nearby.

9
00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:42.590
A passenger may ride with that branch, but it did not cause the widening.

10
00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:47.610
Selection acts on cells, and their DNA marks spread with them.

11
00:00:48.583 --> 00:00:50.623
One route changes an accelerator.

12
00:00:51.963 --> 00:00:56.083
A proto-oncogene normally promotes growth when signals call for it.

13
00:00:57.183 --> 00:01:00.603
A gain-of-function change can make an oncogene overactive.

14
00:01:02.143 --> 00:01:04.903
Picture two gene copies, with one switch locked on.

15
00:01:06.063 --> 00:01:10.063
One altered allele is usually enough to change the cell's behavior.

16
00:01:11.483 --> 00:01:15.703
Extra gene copies or a translocation can also activate this route.

17
00:01:16.767 --> 00:01:18.647
The other route changes a brake.

18
00:01:20.047 --> 00:01:21.727
Here are two tumor-suppressor copies.

19
00:01:23.087 --> 00:01:26.767
One turns red and loses function, while the other remains green.

20
00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:32.667
Predict: does the cell lose the brake now, or can the working copy still hold it?

21
00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:42.010
Usually, one working copy still provides enough brake.

22
00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.890
A second loss removes that remaining function.

23
00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:51.990
This can give the cell a growth or survival advantage, so its branch may expand.

24
00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:55.430
It does not by itself guarantee cancer.

25
00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:01.030
Tumor-suppressor changes are therefore usually recessive within one cell.

26
00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.770
Later, the text adds exceptions to these rules.

27
00:02:05.933 --> 00:02:08.773
RB1 makes the two-hit logic visible.

28
00:02:10.013 --> 00:02:12.433
A child may inherit one nonworking copy.

29
00:02:13.853 --> 00:02:18.513
Gold first-hit badges then appear in every cell, including every retinal cell.

30
00:02:19.733 --> 00:02:23.713
That inheritance raises risk, but it does not make every cell a tumor.

31
00:02:25.133 --> 00:02:27.053
Each cell still has one working copy.

32
00:02:27.893 --> 00:02:31.833
This germline first hit can also pass to the next generation.

33
00:02:33.017 --> 00:02:37.817
A rare somatic second hit removes the working copy in one retinal cell.

34
00:02:39.137 --> 00:02:40.977
Only that cell's descendants expand.

35
00:02:42.337 --> 00:02:47.197
Without an inherited first hit, both hits must arise in the same body-cell lineage.

36
00:02:48.597 --> 00:02:53.797
This is why inherited risk can look dominant in a family while the change remains

37
00:02:53.797 --> 00:02:55.117
recessive within a cell.

38
00:02:56.150 --> 00:03:01.690
Cancer is genetic because altered DNA passes through dividing cell lineages.

39
00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:07.810
It is usually not inherited because most relevant changes arise somatically.

40
00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:12.330
The route may activate an accelerator or remove a brake.

41
00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:18.250
Cell lineages expand when their DNA changes improve growth or survival.

42
00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:23.890
Every change a lineage carries, driver or passenger, spreads with it.
